/*
Problem: Merge Intervals (LC 56)
Topic: Greedy / Sorting

--------------------------------------------------

Idea:

Sort all intervals according to their
starting time.

Maintain one current interval.

For every next interval:

• If it overlaps with the current
  interval, merge them by extending
  the ending point.

• Otherwise, store the current
  interval and start a new one.

--------------------------------------------------

Algorithm:

1. Sort intervals by starting point.

2. Initialize current interval as the
   first interval.

3. Traverse the remaining intervals.

4. If:

   currentEnd >= nextStart

   merge them.

5. Otherwise:

   • Store current interval.

   • Update current interval.

6. Push the last interval.

7. Return the answer.

--------------------------------------------------

Example:

Input:

[[1,3],[2,6],[8,10],[15,18]]

Sorted:

[[1,3],[2,6],[8,10],[15,18]]

Merge:

[1,3] + [2,6]

↓

[1,6]

Answer:

[[1,6],[8,10],[15,18]]

--------------------------------------------------

Example:

Input:

[[1,4],[4,5]]

Since:

4 >= 4

They overlap.

Merged:

[1,5]

Answer:

[[1,5]]

--------------------------------------------------

Why It Works?

After sorting, every interval that can
overlap with the current interval
appears immediately after it.

Thus, a single linear scan is enough
to merge all overlapping intervals.

--------------------------------------------------

Time : O(n log n)

Sorting dominates.

Traversal is O(n).

Space : O(n)

Answer vector stores merged intervals.

--------------------------------------------------

Pattern:

Greedy + Sorting
*/

class Solution {
public:

    static bool paircmp(vector<int>& a,
                        vector<int>& b) {

        return a[0] < b[0];
    }

    vector<vector<int>> merge(
        vector<vector<int>>& intervals) {

        vector<vector<int>> ans;

        sort(intervals.begin(),
             intervals.end(),
             paircmp);

        vector<int> curr = intervals[0];

        for (int i = 1; i < intervals.size(); i++) {

            // Overlapping intervals
            if (curr[1] >= intervals[i][0]) {

                curr[1] = max(curr[1],
                              intervals[i][1]);
            }

            // Non-overlapping interval
            else {

                ans.push_back(curr);
                curr = intervals[i];
            }
        }

        // Store the last interval
        ans.push_back(curr);

        return ans;
    }
};
